  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'PAPER_EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in software development, leveraging large language models to autonomously generate, execute, and debug code. As these agents become increasingly integrated into critical software workflows, their security and reliability have emerged as paramount concerns. This paper provides a comprehensive survey of recent advances in code agents, with a particular focus on red-teaming methodologies for evaluating their safety and robustness. We examine the emerging field of code agent red-teaming, analyzing benchmarks and attack vectors designed to stress-test agent behavior. Our work synthesizes findings from recent literature, including the RedCode benchmark [1], which provides a comprehensive evaluation framework for risky code execution and generation, and draws on established machine learning principles for robust system design [2]. We identify key research directions for improving code agent security and propose a framework for systematic red-teaming evaluation.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents, powered by large language models (LLMs), have revolutionized software development by enabling autonomous code generation, modification, and debugging. Unlike traditional automated tools, code agents can reason about code semantics, understand context, and interact with execution environments in dynamic ways. This capability has led to widespread adoption in software engineering workflows, from automated testing to continuous integration and deployment pipelines.\n\nHowever, the same capabilities that make code agents valuable also introduce significant security risks. Code agents execute arbitrary code, have access to system resources, and can make decisions based on generated code. This creates potential attack vectors where malicious inputs could cause harmful code execution, data exfiltration, or system compromise. The risk is particularly acute in production environments where code agents are deployed at scale.\n\nRed-teaming, a methodology borrowed from cybersecurity that involves adversarial testing of systems, has emerged as a critical approach for evaluating code agent safety. By systematically probing code agents with adversarial inputs, researchers and practitioners can identify vulnerabilities before they are exploited in production. This paper surveys the state-of-the-art in code agent red-teaming, examining benchmarks, attack methodologies, and evaluation frameworks.\n\nThe rest of this paper is organized as follows: Section 2 reviews related work in code agents, red-teaming, and relevant benchmarks. Section 3 presents our analysis of current challenges and research directions. Section 4 concludes with a summary of findings and future work.\n\n## Related Work\n\nThe field of code agents has seen rapid development in recent years, with applications spanning automated programming, code generation, and software engineering tasks. Early work in program synthesis [2] laid foundational principles for machine learning approaches to code generation, establishing methods for training models on program structures and semantics.\n\nAs code agents gained capabilities, researchers recognized the need for rigorous safety evaluation. The RedCode benchmark [1] represents a significant contribution to this effort, providing a comprehensive framework for evaluating risky code execution and generation in code agents. RedCode systematically assesses agent behavior across diverse attack scenarios, including malicious code generation, vulnerability exploitation, and unsafe code patterns. The benchmark addresses gaps in existing evaluation methodologies by focusing on real-world security concerns specific to code execution environments.\n\nRecent work has expanded red-teaming methodologies to cover various aspects of agent safety. Automatic red-teaming agents have been developed to systematically probe code agents against diverse attack vectors [1], enabling scalable security evaluation. These approaches leverage adversarial prompting, input perturbation, and iterative refinement to discover vulnerabilities that might be missed by manual testing.\n\nThe principles underlying robust code agent design draw from established machine learning research, including techniques for model robustness and generalization [2]. These methods provide theoretical foundations for developing agents that maintain safety guarantees under adversarial conditions.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nCode agents represent both a powerful tool and a security challenge for software engineering. As these systems become more capable and widely deployed, rigorous red-teaming evaluation becomes essential for ensuring safe operation. This paper has surveyed advances in code agent safety, highlighting the importance of comprehensive benchmarks like RedCode and drawing on established machine learning principles for robust system design.\n\nFuture research directions include developing standardized red-teaming protocols, creating diverse and representative benchmark suites, and building defense mechanisms that can automatically mitigate discovered vulnerabilities. The interplay between code agent capabilities and security must continue to be a primary focus of research and development efforts.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Xun Liu, Chulin Xie, et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\n\n[2] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, et al. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\nPAPER_EOF"
}'}

